  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA short wave trough is slowly exiting the area out toward the\neast and will provide chances for rain and a possible \nthunderstorm or two along the mountains the rest of the\nafternoon. Rain should be light in nature and the thunderstorm\npotential is low, however if activity occurs they will be sub- \nsevere. Clouds then clear out for the overnight and chances for \nvalley fog may become apparent where areas decouple. Outside the\nvalleys surface wind should be elevated enough to keep fog from\nforming.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nDry for Sunday morning and then a more potent cold frontal \nboundary will impact the area from the northwest. Chances for \nshowers and possible isolated thunderstorms is expected ahead of\nthe boundary during the afternoon and evening. Modest shear and\nlimited instability will keep thunderstorm activity below \nsevere criteria. Some beneficial rainfall will occur along the \nmountains into Monday as the front slowly exits by late morning\nor early afternoon. However, the rest of the area will not see \nmuch in the way of QPF. This will unfortunately not affect the \ndrought status at all. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nA cooler airmass will provide a cooler start to the new work \nweek as high pressure builds in for Tuesday. For Tuesday \nmorning, frost and freeze will be possible along the northeast \nmountains and possible elsewhere in the counties just outside \nthe mountains. Winds will be calm and skies will be clear for \nample radiational cooling which will allow frost to develop ovr\nmuch of the area and also promote freezing temperatures in the \nhighest elevations. Across the rest of the lowlands it is \npossible to have patchy frost in areas of known cold spots or in\ndeeper protected valleys. This potential could threaten \nsensitive vegetation if left unprotected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...\n\nHigh pressure retreats by midweek as a clipper type system\ntraverses toward the area and impacts us with possible \nthunderstorm activity. This feature may promote some stronger \nstorm development as southerly flow returns with a more \nmoisture flux and instability to work with as a cold front is \nforecast to sweep through the area during the afternoon and \nevening during peak heating.",
